-> Why the wide range of Cloud Run?
If services run continuously like now: ~$338/month (cost doubles!)
Realistic middle ground: ~$380-420/month (27-67 MORE expensive)

Cloud Run Isn't Cheaper for Always-On Services. They charge per vCPU-second and GB-second. It's cheaper ONLY if the services:

Scale to zero during low traffic
Have sporadic/bursty usage patterns
Can tolerate cold starts (200ms-2s delay)

Our reality:
Our services need to stay live for user experience as we head into alpha 2.0
We're running services 24/7 anyway

Result: We'd pay 2x MORE on Cloud Run unless we heavily optimize


Migration Effort:
Team impact: High - diverts from Alpha 2.0 work

What would need to be done:
Rewrite all deployment workflows:
Convert  GitHub Actions workflows
Learn Cloud Build or adapt GitHub Actions for GCP
Recreate IAM roles and permissions


Reconfigure all 3 services
Adapt be-app for Cloud Run
Adapt botai for Cloud Run
Migrate fe-web to Firebase Hosting
Update all environment variables
Configure scaling, concurrency, timeouts

Rebuild monitoring
Recreate CloudWatch dashboards in Cloud Monitoring
Set up alerts to Google Chat
Migrate Lambda monitoring functions to Cloud Functions

Risk: We're close to Alpha 2.0 - this would be a distraction

Summary for Decision Making
Migration to Firebase/GCP:
Cost savings: minimal to negative (-6% best case, +8-38% realistic/worst)
Effort: high
Timing: bad (approaching Alpha 2.0)
Risk: high (service disruptions, learning curve)
Benefit: Only if we have strategic reasons beyond cost

Key Takeaways
Firebase/GCP is NOT cheaper for always-on services like ours

Cloud Run costs 2x more than Fargate if services don't scale to zero
Our biggest AWS costs (ECS, ECR) have no savings in GCP
Migration effort is massive


Recommendation
Immediate action: Focus on AWS monitoring the optimization made to cut costs. (Reducing resource allocation for our ECS clusters) which seems to have worked for the previous month in comparison to the billing for October.
Migration decision: Revisit after Alpha 2.0 launch and AWS optimization continued monitoring results

